WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.774
This video covers interrupts: the fast part, the slow part, and how data moves with DMA. We'll follow an HDMI hot plug interrupt on an I two C bridge, step by step.

00:00:11.174 --> 00:00:21.286
IRQ / interrupt: Interrupt request: a hardware signal that makes the processor stop and handle an event right now. Like: A doorbell: you stop what you are doing and answer it.

00:00:21.586 --> 00:00:32.755
DMA: Direct memory access: a hardware helper that copies data to memory without the processor doing each byte. Like: A conveyor belt that moves boxes while the worker does something else.

00:00:33.055 --> 00:00:41.507
HDMI: The common cable standard for sending video and audio to TVs and monitors. Like: A garden hose for pictures and sound.

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:54.764
EDID / HPD: EDID is the screen's description of what it can show; hot-plug detect (HPD) is the wire that says a screen is connected. Like: A guest's dietary card (EDID) and the doorbell that says they arrived (HPD).

00:00:55.064 --> 00:01:06.419
I2C: A simple two-wire bus that lets a processor talk to small chips like sensors and HDMI bridges. Like: A classroom where the teacher calls each student by number and they answer one at a time.

00:01:07.519 --> 00:01:11.988
A user presses mute on the bar, and the video freezes for a split second.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:17.358
The interrupt handler for the button was reading a chip over a slow bus while everything else waited.

00:01:17.608 --> 00:01:21.649
Let's learn how to split interrupt work so the fast part stays fast.

00:01:22.699 --> 00:01:24.266
An interrupt is a doorbell.

00:01:24.666 --> 00:01:31.307
The top half is answering the door and signing for the package. It must take seconds, because other visitors may be waiting.

00:01:31.707 --> 00:01:36.084
ISR / top half: Interrupt service routine: the tiny, fast handler that runs the moment the interrupt fires. Like: Answering the doorbell just long enough to take the parcel.

00:01:36.384 --> 00:01:41.817
The bottom half is unpacking later in the kitchen, where you can take your time and even make phone calls.

00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:47.337
Workqueue / bottom half: A kernel queue that runs deferred work later in a normal context where sleeping is allowed. Like: Putting the parcel on the table to open properly after you have closed the door.

00:01:47.637 --> 00:01:52.560
And DMA is a conveyor belt that loads the shelves by itself, while you do other work.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.457
Words.

00:01:54.857 --> 00:02:02.008
The top half is the hard interrupt handler. It runs with that interrupt masked, must be very short, and cannot sleep.

00:02:02.408 --> 00:02:08.956
A threaded interrupt moves the slow part into a kernel thread, which may sleep, for example to talk over I two C.

00:02:09.356 --> 00:02:16.078
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:02:16.328 --> 00:02:20.125
A workqueue runs deferred functions later, in normal process context.

00:02:20.525 --> 00:02:26.167
DMA, direct memory access, lets hardware copy data into memory without the CPU.

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:31.989
A spinlock is a lock that busy waits instead of sleeping, so it's safe in interrupt context.

00:02:33.189 --> 00:02:35.999
Let's follow a hot plug, step by step.

00:02:36.399 --> 00:02:40.219
A monitor is connected. The bridge chip pulls its interrupt line low.

00:02:40.619 --> 00:02:44.601
The interrupt controller masks that line, and calls the hard handler.

00:02:45.001 --> 00:02:53.581
Because the bridge sits on I two C, the hard handler can't read it. Reading I two C sleeps. So it simply returns I R Q WAKE THREAD.

00:02:53.981 --> 00:02:55.989
The core wakes the interrupt thread.

00:02:56.389 --> 00:03:02.101
The thread reads the bridge's status register over I two C. This sleeps, and that's fine here.

00:03:02.501 --> 00:03:06.658
It writes to clear the status, so the chip releases the interrupt line.

00:03:07.058 --> 00:03:12.468
It tells the display core that a monitor arrived, so the EDID can be read and a mode chosen.

00:03:12.868 --> 00:03:24.129
Finally it returns handled, and only now is the line unmasked. That's what the oneshot flag guarantees. Without it, a level interrupt would fire again and again before the thread could clear it.

00:03:25.329 --> 00:03:27.338
Here's what that looks like in code.

00:03:27.738 --> 00:03:31.442
The thread function reads the status through regmap. It may sleep.

00:03:31.842 --> 00:03:42.825
regmap: A kernel helper that reads and writes a chip's registers over I2C or SPI with caching. Like: A shared notebook of a chip's settings so you do not re-ask the chip every time.

00:03:43.125 --> 00:03:44.727
Then it clears the status.

00:03:45.127 --> 00:03:49.086
If the hot plug bit changed, it notifies the display core.

00:03:49.486 --> 00:03:58.832
Notice the request call passes null for the hard handler. The kernel supplies a default one that just wakes the thread, and oneshot keeps the line masked until the thread returns.

00:04:00.032 --> 00:04:01.390
Which lock to use?

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.645
Data shared with a hard interrupt handler needs spin lock irq save.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:14.659
Spinlock / mutex: Locks that stop two pieces of code changing the same data at once; a spinlock waits busily, a mutex sleeps. Like: A single bathroom key: a spinlock is waiting at the door, a mutex is taking a seat until called.

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:18.555
Data touched only from process context can use a mutex.

00:04:18.955 --> 00:04:21.625
Single counters and flags can be atomics.

00:04:22.025 --> 00:04:27.017
And turn on lockdep in debug builds. It reports deadlock risks before they ever happen.

00:04:28.217 --> 00:04:30.400
Now DMA, step by step.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.754
The driver maps a buffer for the device. On ARM, this cleans or invalidates the CPU caches for that memory.

00:04:38.154 --> 00:04:42.775
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:04:43.025 --> 00:04:47.205
It hands the bus address to the hardware, usually through a descriptor ring.

00:04:47.605 --> 00:04:50.844
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:04:51.094 --> 00:04:54.844
The hardware writes the frame into memory, without the CPU.

00:04:55.244 --> 00:04:57.125
A completion interrupt arrives.

00:04:57.525 --> 00:05:03.400
Before reading, the driver syncs the buffer for the CPU, which throws away any stale cache lines.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:06.330
Now the CPU sees the correct data.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:17.714
Then it syncs back for the device, and reuses the buffer. Skip these syncs, and on ARM you'll see green stripes or stale lines in video, even though it works perfectly on x86.

00:05:18.914 --> 00:05:24.765
Question for you: Why can't you read an I2C chip directly inside the top-half interrupt handler?

00:05:30.065 --> 00:05:40.468
Answer: The top half runs with interrupts blocked and must never sleep, but an I2C transfer can sleep and is slow. Defer it to a threaded interrupt or a workqueue.

00:05:40.768 --> 00:05:46.793
Threaded IRQ: An interrupt whose slow part runs in its own kernel thread so it can sleep and talk over I2C. Like: A receptionist who takes a message, then a colleague phones back properly.

00:05:47.893 --> 00:05:48.590
Recap.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.577
The top half acknowledges and defers. It never sleeps.

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:56.971
Devices on I two C or SPI need a threaded interrupt with oneshot.

00:05:57.371 --> 00:06:06.334
SPI: A fast four-wire bus for chips like flash memory and displays. Like: A dedicated phone line to each chip instead of a shared classroom.

00:06:06.634 --> 00:06:09.757
Choose the lock by the context that touches the data.

00:06:10.157 --> 00:06:14.337
Always use the DMA API, and sync caches on ARM.

00:06:14.737 --> 00:06:22.806
And tear down in order: disable the source, free the interrupt, cancel work, then free memory. Lab three practices exactly that.
